  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Consider the Diophantine equation $7x+9y=11.$ The integer pair $(\\overset{\u223c}{x},\\overset{\u223c}{y})$ is the solution with the smallest possible positive value of $x.$ What is the value of $\\overset{\u223c}{x}?$",
  "worked_solution": "We have the linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere $a=7,$ $b=9,$ and $c=11.$ We're given that the equation has solutions.\n\nNotice that our equation can't be solved for either of the two variables using integer coefficients. So, let's find the linear representation of $\\text{gcd}(7,9)$ using the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}9 & = & 7\\cdot 1 & + & 2 \\\\  & \u2199 &  & \u2199 &  \\\\ 7 & = & 2\\cdot 3 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =9-7 \\\\ 1 & =7-2\\cdot 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =7-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =7-3(9-7) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 4+(-3)\\cdot 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ and\n\n$$\n7\\cdot 4+9\\cdot (-3)=1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this equation by $11,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\cdot 4+9\\cdot (-3) & =1 \\\\ 7\\cdot 4\\cdot 11+9\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 11 & =11 \\\\ 7\\cdot 44+9\\cdot (-33) & =11,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $(x,y)=(44,-33)$ is a solution to our Diophantine equation.\n\nNow, the general solution is\n\n$$\n(44+\\frac{b}{d}t,\\,-33-\\frac{a}{d}t)=(44+9t,\\,-33-7t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\u2026\\,.$\n\nThe smallest possible positive value of $x=44+9t$ is attained when $t=-4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{\u223c}{x}=44+9\\cdot (-4)=8.\n$$",
